{"chunk_id": "d7c43ac42cf6", "text": "Sikhandini, daughter of King Drupada, is overcome with shame and guilt after realizing that her concealed female birth has brought political danger to her parents. Determined to end her life, she retreats into a forbidden forest inhabited by the Yaksha Sthunakarna. Moved by her plight, the Yaksha offers a magical bargain: he will lend her his manhood temporarily if she promises to return it later. They exchange sexes, and Sikhandini returns to her city as Sikhandin, a man. When the king of the Dasarnakas accuses Drupada of deception for marrying his daughter to a supposed woman, Drupada insists Sikhandin is male. The Dasarnaka king sends women to verify the truth, and their testimony confirms Sikhandin\u2019s masculinity, restoring peace and honor to Drupada\u2019s house. The episode establishes Sikhandin\u2019s transformed identity, crucial for later events in the Mahabharata.\nSikhandini gains temporary manhood from the Yaksha Sthunakarna to save her family and prove herself male, averting conflict with the Dasarna king.\nThemes: gender transformation, divine intervention, honor and deception, family duty, political conflict", "vector": [0.0018238587072119117, -0.023927409201860428, 0.0014575712848454714, 0.008602281101047993, -0.014334889128804207, 0.014995048753917217, -0.02937035635113716, -0.005200439132750034, -0.013641048222780228, -0.026123449206352234, 0.02980148047208786, 0.006311932113021612, 0.024533677846193314, 0.024735767394304276, 0.01349284965544939, 0.00954536534845829, 0.04720813035964966, -0.008043166249990463, 0.018039865419268608, -0.03403189033269882, -0.017379706725478172, 0.009282649494707584, 0.0011350699933245778, 0.019710473716259003, -0.03686114400625229, 0.01968352682888508, 0.017420124262571335, -0.014860322698950768, 0.012024331837892532, -0.018780861049890518, -0.02720126137137413, -0.0061502605676651, 0.0031829113140702248, -0.008541653864085674, -0.0339510552585125, 0.0122196851298213, -0.0095